Polycythaemia Vera
Haematology & Oncology
Aquagenic pruritus, Plethoric/ruddy complexion, Unexplained thrombosis, Elevated haemoglobin with JAK2 mutation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HMO-038
Reason for Visit: Sudden onset of a severe, unremitting headache, intense dizziness, and visual disturbances requiring urgent assessment for hyperviscosity syndrome or acute stroke.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient experiencing a sudden, crushing headache, profound tinnitus, and transient blurred vision, accompanied by a bright red face. Urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to clinically rule out an acute cerebrovascular event or severe hyperviscosity crisis, which posed an immediate risk of major arterial thrombosis if not assessed and managed prior to normal clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is pounding relentlessly, the room is spinning, and my vision keeps blurring in and out. My face feels like it is on fire."
History of Present Illness: The 55-year-old patient reports an insidious onset of generalized severe fatigue, systemic lethargy, and decreased exercise tolerance over the past 6 months. They detail a highly specific symptom of aquagenic pruritus—severe, unrelenting itching explicitly triggered by contact with warm water during showers, lasting for up to an hour afterward. Over the past 4 hours, they developed a chronic unremitting headache, severe dizziness, and profound tinnitus. They also experienced a transient visual disturbance (blurring) lasting 15 minutes. They complain of a constant feeling of fullness in the upper left abdomen and intense burning pain in their hands (erythromelalgia).
Functional Status: Lying flat in a dark room; unable to stand due to severe vertigo and headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of a catastrophic stroke overnight.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 165/95 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert adult exhibiting a markedly plethoric, intensely ruddy (florid red) facial complexion. Conjunctivae are severely injected (bloodshot).
Physical Exam:
Neurological: Cranial nerves II-XII are grossly intact. No focal sensory or motor deficits in the limbs. Finger-to-nose testing is normal. Visual fields are currently intact.
Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Bounding peripheral pulses. Hypertension noted.
Abdomen: Soft. Firm splenomegaly is palpable 3 cm below the left costal margin, causing localized fullness.
Extremities: Bilateral palms and soles are intensely erythematous and warm to the touch (erythromelalgia). No pitting oedema.
Environmental Observations: Room is kept cool and dark to manage the headache.
Medication Reconciliation: Took ibuprofen 400mg with absolute zero effect on the headache.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Polycythaemia Vera - Status: Exacerbated (Severe Hyperviscosity Syndrome)
Differentials:

Acute Ischaemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the complete absence of focal motor or sensory deficits, resolving visual changes, and the classic systemic background of aquagenic pruritus and plethora.

Essential Hypertension - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the distinctly plethoric face, injected conjunctivae, palpable splenomegaly, and specific aquagenic itch, which unequivocally point to a myeloproliferative disorder.

Mast Cell Activation Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the lack of hives/urticaria, the presence of splenomegaly, and the severe hyperviscosity symptoms (headache/tinnitus), which are driven by red cell mass, not histamine release alone.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the pathognomonic clinical features of Polycythaemia Vera: a ruddy plethoric complexion, aquagenic pruritus, erythromelalgia, and palpable splenomegaly. The acute presentation of a severe unremitting headache, intense dizziness, tinnitus, and transient visual blurring represents a critical hyperviscosity crisis. The massively expanded red blood cell mass is causing 'sludging' of blood flow in the cerebral microvasculature. Delaying assessment until normal hours would expose the patient to an unacceptably high risk of irreversible massive cerebral infarction (stroke) or retinal vein occlusion overnight. The urgent home visit was essential to physically rule out an established stroke and arrange immediate hospital transfer for emergency therapeutic venesection (phlebotomy).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered immediate oral Aspirin 300mg stat to rapidly inhibit platelet aggregation and reduce the immediate risk of microvascular thrombosis. Immediate referral and ambulance transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent full blood count (anticipating markedly elevated haemoglobin and haematocrit) and emergency therapeutic venesection to acutely lower the blood viscosity.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain lying completely flat and still in the dark room until the ambulance arrives. By eliminating physical exertion and sensory input, you lower your blood pressure slightly and reduce the oxygen demand on your struggling brain.

Avoid taking hot showers or baths completely. By strictly avoiding warm water contact on the skin, you prevent the massive, localized histamine release that triggers the agonizing, uncontrollable itching (aquagenic pruritus).
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department assessment is required. Call 000 if you suddenly develop weakness on one side of your face or body, slurred speech, or a complete loss of vision in one eye prior to paramedic arrival.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The specific combination of a red complexion, water-triggered itching, and severe headache strongly suggests your blood is far too thick; urgent hospital blood tests and a genetic test (JAK2) are required to confirm this.
Clinical Course: The thick blood is moving sluggishly through your brain, causing the headache and dizziness. The hospital will likely need to urgently remove a unit of blood (like a blood donation) to immediately thin it out and relieve the pressure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk. The dizziness puts you at severe risk of a fall. Wait for the paramedics to assist you.

